---

Third-party components:

Kokoro-82M (text to speech model weights)
  https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M
  Apache License 2.0

Lucide icons (the Icons picker in the editor, and the icons a course holds)
  https://lucide.dev
  ISC License, Copyright (c) Lucide Icons and Contributors, with some icons derived from the
  Feather project (MIT License). The full text is in third-party/lucide-LICENSE.txt. The
  editor's icon list is editor/web/icons/lucide.json, made by tools/make_icon_catalogue.py.
  A course that uses an icon carries the notice: its credits page names Lucide, and the
  published package includes the licence text as licenses/lucide.txt.

Speech engine (installed separately by the user; not included, bundled or hosted by OpenStage Author)
  Spoken narration is made by a speech engine that the user installs on their own computer
  and that OpenStage Author runs as a separate program. That engine may include components
  under the GNU General Public License, such as phonemizer-fork and espeak-ng. OpenStage
  Author does not include, bundle, or host them. They keep their own licences.

AI services and voice plugins (connected by the user; not included, bundled or hosted by OpenStage Author)
  The editor can make voices and pictures with OpenAI (https://openai.com), and can run
  speech models the user installs as plugins. OpenStage Author includes no service, model,
  key or account of theirs. The user supplies their
  own key, and the service's own terms and privacy rules apply to what is sent to it and to what
  it makes. A plugin is a separate program with its own licence. The example plugin in
  examples/plugins is part of OpenStage Author (Apache License 2.0). No component of these
  services is distributed with a course; a course credits the services that made its material.

Speech recognition for captions (built or downloaded by the user; not included, bundled or hosted by OpenStage Author)
  Captions from a recorded voice use whisper.cpp (https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp, MIT License, Copyright (c) 2023-2026 The ggml authors), which the user builds with
  BUILD SPEECH RECOGNISER.bat and which the editor runs as a separate program, and a Whisper speech model that the user chooses to download from
  https://huggingface.co/ggerganov/whisper.cpp (ggml-small.en.bin or ggml-base.en.bin; OpenAI's Whisper weights, which OpenAI's repository
  https://github.com/openai/whisper licenses under the MIT License, Copyright (c) 2022 OpenAI; the Hugging Face card of the related whisper-small model says Apache License 2.0; both
  are permissive). Neither is part of a course. The build copies whisper.cpp's licence into the speech folder as whisper.cpp-LICENSE.txt.

OpenStage Capture (screen recording; the capture/, capture-edit/ and capture-timeline/ folders)
  The recorder is a Rust program built from the crates listed, each with its licence, in
  capture/THIRD-PARTY.md (every one MIT, Apache-2.0 or similarly permissive; the rule checker
  capture/tools/check_rules.py fails the build otherwise). It records through services that are
  part of Windows (screen capture, sound, camera and video encoding); none of that is distributed
  with it. It contains no FFmpeg. The edit model and the editing screens are TypeScript with no
  outside code. The audit of other recorders, and why none of their code is used, is in
  docs/decisions/0001-screen-capture.md.

The editor program (the editor/ folder) and the publishing tools
  The editor is a Tauri program written in Rust, with a web side in TypeScript. Its 255 Rust crates, and the JavaScript packages that its web side and the
  publishing tools use (@tauri-apps/api and its two plugins, fflate, esbuild), are listed with their licences in editor/THIRD-PARTY.md, which
  tools/check_licences.py writes and checks on request. Every licence in it is permissive (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib, Unicode, CC0 and the like)
  except the Mozilla Public License 2.0, which five of the crates carry (cssparser, cssparser-macros, dtoa-short, option-ext, selectors; they come with
  Tauri's web view layer). That licence applies file by file; those crates are used exactly as published, and their source is on crates.io. The check
  fails if a sixth appears, or if anything with a copyleft or unknown licence does. A program or package that is given to other people carries
  the licence texts, which tools/check_licences.py --texts collects.
  The editor shows its windows with Microsoft's WebView2, which is part of Windows 11 and is installed separately on older Windows. OpenStage Author does
  not include it.

Any further bundled model or library is listed here with its licence before
it ships. Open weights are not automatically open source: check each one.
